{"content":[{"c":["AUGUSTUS’S BAND*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Roman military unit mentioned in ",{"href":"?bref=Acts.27.1","r":"Acts.27.1","rawR":"Acts.27.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.27.1","value":"Acts.27.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 27:1"}," (",{"c":["kjv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},"). Julius, the centurion who had custody of the apostle Paul on the way to Rome, was a member of Augustus’s Band. The Greek word translated “band” normally meant a Roman cohort or force of two cohorts. Some scholars, assuming that Julius was in command of that unit, have wondered why an officer normally commanding a century (100 men) should be in charge of 500 to 1,000. Perhaps Julius was not in command of the whole unit, or it was not a regular cohort (tenth part of a Roman legion) but a special courier or guard detachment."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=Warfare_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Warfare_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Warfare_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Warfare"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"AugustussBand","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Acts 27:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Acts.27.1","rawQueryValue":"Acts.27.1","sourceRefExact":"Acts.27.1"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ef0302679806f8c612bfc6b71c24bd279f0038dd1bf75c62ac6330a352f8a418","sourceItemName":"AugustussBand","sourceItemOrder":618,"sourceMember":"Articles/A.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"AugustussBand","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Augustus’s Band*","title":"Augustus’s Band*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}